<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 13: Rotational Kinematics and Angular Momentum</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 1.4.2, 1.6 (components of cross products), section
      8.2 (up to example 8.3), and sections 7.1 and 7.2. You should do the reading roughly in this order. 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Draw a picture showing the equivalence of the <q>corkscrew rule</q> to the following method to find
            the direction of <m>\vec{A}\times\vec{B}</m>: put the index finger of your right hand in the direction 
            of <m>\vec{A}</m>, then have your middle finger point in the direction of <m>\vec{B}</m>. The
            cross-product <m>\vec{A}\times\vec{B}</m> then points in the direction of your thumb.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	The definition of angular velocity in terms of derivatives of angles as shown in K&amp;K is nice but not 
            really correct (you’ll fix this in 4011). For now, show that for all the examples in 8.2 you can find 
            the direction of <m>\vec{\omega}</m> in the following way. Curl your right hand in the direction of 
            rotation, and then <m>\vec{\omega}</m>points along your thumb. Its magnitude is given by the instantaneous
            <m>\frac{d\theta}{dt}</m> about the axis of rotation (this part is a definition, don't show it).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          			Check that for either choice of origin in example 7.2, the relationship 
                <m>~{\vec{v}=\vec{\omega}\times\vec{r}}</m>. Use the rule from the above problem to find <m>\vec{\omega}</m>
                Lesson: if a vector obeys <m>~{\frac{d\vec{A}}{dt}=\vec{\omega}\times\vec{A}}</m>, then that vector is
                rotating with constant angular velocity about the axis determined by <m>\vec{\omega}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		Verify, using the right hand rule (draw some pictures!) that the directions of 
              <m>\vec{L}_B</m> in example 7.2 are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          
          	It may seem weird that <m>\vec{L}</m> in the previous example has a horizontal component but think 
            about the following questions. 
            
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                If you didn't know the particle was in a conical pendulum, and all you had was a picture showing 
                you <m>\vec{r},\vec{p}</m>, could you say whether the particle was rotating along the z axis or the x axis?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                 In which sense, then, does the direction of <m>\vec{L}</m> <q>splits the difference</q> or, 
                 rather, accounts for both possibilities?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>

            
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
